{{!--
	ContentBody.mustache -- the page content region, and the skin's preserved DOM contract.

	Every id and every class in the markup below is core's. The Blitzy redesign is total; the
	DOM contract underneath it is not. Gadgets, user scripts, VisualEditor, core's own
	JavaScript and the editor-facing tooling core protects all address this region through
	the names emitted here, so rule R2 requires each of them to survive the redesign with
	unchanged semantics. tests/playwright/domContract.spec.ts asserts every one against a
	fetched page, which is Gate 8's second check. The only things this file adds are two
	class names, which R2 explicitly permits alongside core's.

	The redesign consequently happens entirely in the stylesheets. skins.blitzy.styles hangs
	the content measure, surface and vertical rhythm on .blitzy-content-body and
	.blitzy-content-body__inner, while components/Prose.less, Tables.less and Infobox.less
	reach the parser output through core's own selectors. That licence exists only while this
	file passes the parser output through unaltered, which is why nothing below rewrites,
	re-wraps or re-classes it (R7).

	INVOCATION

	skin.mustache includes this file as a bare ContentBody partial reference at root context.
	No section pushes a context, so every key named below is resolved from the root of the
	template data and this file must not assume otherwise. Partials resolve by filename, so
	the name ContentBody.mustache is itself part of the contract.

	Exactly one partial is included from here, CategoryFooter, and it is included inside
	div#bodyContent. TableOfContents is deliberately not included: it is a sibling of this
	partial inside skin.mustache's content layout, so that the stylesheet can stick it beside
	the content at 1024px and reorder it below at narrower widths, and it has to be entered
	with data-toc as its context, which is not this file's context to supply.

	DATA CONSUMED, all from the root of the template data

	string html-user-language-attributes
		An attribute fragment, not element content. Skin::prepareUserLanguageAttributes()
		returns Html::expandAttributes() of lang and dir, and only when the interface
		language differs from the content language; otherwise the empty string. Every
		attribute in that fragment already carries its own leading space, so the tag sits
		flush against the preceding quote inside the start tag with no space of its own.

	string html-subtitle
		Skin::prepareSubtitle() -- finished HTML, and it brings its own container:
		div#mw-content-subtitle carrying the same lang and dir pair, wrapping div.subpages
		(the subpage breadcrumb) and OutputPage::getSubtitle(), which is where redirect,
		old-revision and similar notices arrive. Never empty of its wrapper, so it is
		emitted unguarded.

	string|null html-undelete-link
		Skin::prepareUndeleteLink() -- div.subpages holding the "view deleted revisions"
		link, or null, which is the case on every ordinary page view. Guarded by a section
		so an ordinary page gets no empty div#contentSub2 rather than a hollow one.

	string|null html-user-message
		The new-talk-page-messages notice. SkinMustache::getTemplateData() has already
		wrapped Skin::getNewtalks() in div.usermessage, which is the selector core's
		interface-user-message skin feature styles, so this file adds no wrapper of its own;
		a second one would nest one warning box inside another. Null when there is no
		notice, and a null interpolation renders as nothing.

	string html-body-content
		The page's finished body region. Skin::wrapHTML() has already wrapped
		OutputPage::getHTML() together with the print footer in
		div#mw-content-text.mw-body-content, so this single key delivers the parser output,
		div.mw-parser-output, every parser-emitted mw- prefixed class, and
		div.printfooter[data-nosnippet]. Emitted whole and untouched.

	string html-after-content
		Skin::afterContentHook() -- div#mw-data-after-content when an extension answers the
		SkinAfterContent hook, and the empty string when none does. Core assigns that id
		itself so it is identical across skins.

	html-categories is consumed by CategoryFooter.mustache rather than here, at the same
	root context, which is why that partial is included and no category markup is authored
	in this file.

	FOUR RULINGS THAT DECIDE WHAT THIS FILE MAY EMIT

	1. The print footer and div#mw-content-text are already inside html-body-content.
	   SkinMustache::getTemplateData() builds div.printfooter[data-nosnippet], with the user
	   language attributes on it, and concatenates it onto the page HTML
	   (includes/Skin/SkinMustache.php L75-83) before passing the pair through
	   Skin::wrapHTML(), which adds the div#mw-content-text.mw-body-content wrapper
	   (includes/Skin/Skin.php L2439-2448). Authoring either here would produce a duplicate
	   id and a second data-nosnippet region -- the likeliest defect in this folder, and one
	   no visual check catches. Neither is authored.

	2. html-subtitle brings its own container, and the double wrapping is correct.
	   Skin::prepareSubtitle() returns div#mw-content-subtitle (includes/Skin/Skin.php
	   L2365-2375). Wrapping it in div#contentSub therefore yields both the legacy
	   #contentSub, which is the editor-facing name, and the modern #mw-content-subtitle,
	   which is what core's interface-subtitle feature styles alongside #contentSub2. Both
	   are required; the nesting is deliberate and is not to be flattened.

	3. div#contentSub is emitted exactly once, and it is emitted here.
	   ContentHeader.mustache deliberately does not emit it. The requirement that the content
	   header sit over the tagline, indicator and subtitle regions is met by visual stacking,
	   not by duplicating elements: a second #contentSub is a duplicate id, which is a
	   blocking R10 failure on all twenty-one captures, and it breaks the contract core
	   annotates on the element. It is never hidden and never removed, not even when it
	   renders empty, because editors are still using it (Wikimedia task T316830).

	4. html-user-language-attributes is a raw attribute string.
	   Skin::prepareUserLanguageAttributes() returns Html::expandAttributes()
	   (includes/Skin/Skin.php L2415-2419), so the triple-brace tag belongs inside a start
	   tag rather than in element content. That is one of the reserved lawful uses of an
	   unescaped tag; escaping it would emit the attribute source as visible text.

	ORDER IS PART OF THE CONTRACT

	The sequence below -- subtitle, undelete link, user message, body content, categories,
	then after-content outside the landmark -- is the order core and every core-conformant
	skin produce, and gadgets that insert siblings relative to these elements depend on it.
	Two placements are load-bearing and easy to get wrong in opposite directions:

	  * The category block goes inside div#bodyContent, immediately after the body content.
	    Lifting it out of the content region is a break no visual check catches, because it
	    still looks right.
	  * html-after-content goes outside the main element. Its position relative to the
	    content region is part of the SkinAfterContent hook's documented contract.

	Nothing here is reordered for layout: the visual order is a stylesheet concern, and
	nothing below is conditionally omitted, since a fidelity assertion against an absent
	element has to fail rather than skip (R11).

	NOT EMITTED HERE, DELIBERATELY

	  no printfooter        already inside html-body-content (ruling 1).
	  no #mw-content-text   already inside html-body-content (ruling 1).
	  no .mw-body-content   core's Skin::wrapHTML() puts that class on #mw-content-text.
	                        Repeating it on #bodyContent would apply the content-body
	                        feature's clearfix and content rules twice over an element that
	                        also contains the subtitle and the categories (R8).
	  no h1                 core's h1#firstHeading arrives through html-title-heading in
	                        ContentHeader.mustache. A second heading, or a second element
	                        carrying that id, would leave the aria-labelledby reference below
	                        ambiguous and disturb heading order (R10).
	  no landmark name      the page carries exactly one main element, so the landmark needs
	                        no accessible name, and role="main" would only restate the
	                        element's own semantics.
	  no tabindex           nothing here is focusable in its own right, and a positive
	                        tabindex would reorder the whole page's focus sequence (R10).
	  no id of this skin's  the two Blitzy hooks are classes. Ids have to be unique
	                        page-wide, and this region renders on every page, so adding none
	                        is the cheapest guarantee against a collision and the cheapest
	                        way to emit nothing that could differ between two identical
	                        requests (R9, R10).
	  no style attribute    every colour, radius and measure resolves in the stylesheets
	                        against tokens.less, so no literal reaches this markup (R3).
	  no directional        no float, align or side-specific attribute. Content flow
	                        direction arrives in core's dir attribute inside
	                        html-user-language-attributes, which is passed through, and every
	                        directional rule stays in the flip-aware stylesheets where the
	                        style pipeline can flip it (R6).
	  no subresource        no image, icon, font or script is referenced. Article images and
	                        editorial hyperlinks inside the parser output are core's and pass
	                        through untouched; the external-origins guard exempts content
	                        links and inspects subresources, of which this file adds none
	                        (R5).
	  no message key        nothing here is worded by this skin, so no key is referenced and
	                        none has to be documented in i18n/qqq.json (Gate 2).
	  no behaviour          no reading-progress indicator, no floating action button, no edit
	                        affordance of this skin's own, no scroll container, no reading
	                        time. Structure only (R12).

	Rendered output, in full: main#content.mw-body.blitzy-content-body wrapping
	div#bodyContent.blitzy-content-body__inner, which holds div#contentSub, optionally
	div#contentSub2, the user message, the body content and the category footer; then core's
	after-content region as a sibling that follows the landmark.
--}}
<main id="content" class="mw-body blitzy-content-body">
	<div id="bodyContent" class="blitzy-content-body__inner" aria-labelledby="firstHeading" data-mw-ve-target-container>
		{{!--
			#contentSub is in use by editors: never hide it, never remove it, and never
			emit a second one. See Wikimedia task T316830.
		--}}
		<div id="contentSub"{{{html-user-language-attributes}}}>{{{html-subtitle}}}</div>
		{{#html-undelete-link}}<div id="contentSub2">{{{.}}}</div>{{/html-undelete-link}}
		{{{html-user-message}}}
		{{{html-body-content}}}
		{{>CategoryFooter}}
	</div>
</main>
{{{html-after-content}}}
